  "text": "An NVMe label tells you something about a storage technology. It does not, by itself, tell you how fast your application will run on a particular VPS plan. Begin with the task, a small static site, an active database, and a built process use storage differently. Ask which operations are actually slowing your own service before turning a label into a buying rule. Next, read the plan documentation for storage capacity and any published input-out of limits. Shared infrastructure, workload patterns and provider policies can affect the result if a useful limit is not documented. Record that gap rather than filling it with an estimated benchmark. Keep disk capacity separate from throughput and latency. A larger disk is not the same promise as faster storage, and one short synthetic result would not describe every application, even if a test had been performed. Our take, compare the stated constraints and the workload together. A clear question for support is more useful than an unsupported ranking of every plan labeled NVMe. This video explains how to evaluate the claim. It does not report a storage benchmark or guarantee a performance difference between providers. The description links to the complete storage guide. Use it to build a short list. Then verify the specific plan documentation, and, where appropriate, run a permitted test for your own workload.",
